const antisthenesSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('recovery', 'A prolific Socratic recovered from other people’s books', [
    'Antisthenes belongs among the major writers around Socrates, yet the evidence reaches us in a badly uneven form. Diogenes Laertius transmits a catalogue filling ten rolls and ranging across speeches, dialogues, education, names, knowledge, ethics, politics, Homer, and natural questions. Only the rhetorical pieces Ajax and Odysseus survive continuously under his name; most philosophical material consists of fragments, summaries, or sayings selected by authors with their own agendas. The modern picture is therefore not an abridged version of an intact Antisthenic system. It is a reconstruction from different genres, dates, and degrees of proximity.',
    'That situation makes two familiar portraits hazardous. Antisthenes cannot simply be reduced to an ascetic who anticipated Diogenes of Sinope, because his literary and linguistic interests are too extensive. Nor can every proposition attributed to “Antisthenes” be combined into a seamless doctrine, because Aristotle, Xenophon, Diogenes Laertius, and later commentators preserve different kinds of evidence. This article treats Xenophon’s Socratic scenes, Aristotle’s reports about argument, the late ethical doxography, and the book catalogue as separate dossiers. Agreement across them can strengthen a reconstruction; one late anecdote cannot settle chronology, authorship, or school membership by itself.',
    'Even “fragment” can obscure these differences. A continuous passage transmitted under Antisthenes’ name, a sentence quoted to make an Aristotelian objection, and a maxim assigned to him in a biographical collection have not survived in the same way. The surrounding witness determines what question the words can answer. A catalogue verifies that ancient librarians or scholars attributed a title; it does not verify every item’s authenticity or contents. This source-sensitive method yields fewer dramatic certainties, but it restores the breadth of an author whom later school history narrowed.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['socrates', 'diogenes'], ['Ajax', 'Odysseus', 'Lost writings and testimonia']),
  section('life-socrates', 'Athens, Socrates, and uncertain biography', [
    'Conventional dates place Antisthenes around 445 to 365 BCE, but these endpoints are reconstructed rather than documentary. Ancient reports make him the son of an Athenian father and a non-Athenian, often Thracian, mother; later writers exploit that ancestry in stories about citizenship, Cynosarges, and social independence. They also make him a student of Gorgias before he joined Socrates. The rhetorical works and Xenophon’s repeated use of Antisthenes make rhetorical training and a prominent place in Socratic literature plausible, but they do not verify every teacher-chain or picturesque journey across Athens attributed by much later biography.',
    'The relationship to Socrates is firmer than the later succession from Socrates through Antisthenes to Diogenes. Xenophon gives Antisthenes substantial speaking roles in the Symposium, presenting him as poor in money but rich in leisure, friendship, and independence, and as a practiced questioner. Plato lists an Antisthenes among those present at Socrates’ death, though identity and what follows from attendance require restraint. These witnesses establish participation in the remembered Socratic circle. They do not show that Antisthenes merely repeated a single historical Socrates, since Socratic authors constructed rival literary inheritances after 399 BCE.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'ethics'], ['socrates', 'gorgias', 'plato'], ['Symposium', 'Phaedo']),
  section('xenophon', 'What Xenophon’s Antisthenes can responsibly show', [
    'In Symposium 4, Antisthenes calls his poverty wealth because few possessions leave him content, free, and able to spend time with people he values. The scene does not deliver a formal treatise on self-sufficiency, but it connects material simplicity with control of desire and freedom from compulsory acquisition. His exchanges also display cross-examination, teasing, matchmaking, and a capacity to move between comic performance and moral scrutiny. This is a more socially embedded figure than the solitary Cynic stereotype. Poverty matters not as deprivation for its own sake but as a practical test of whether wants command the person.',
    'Xenophon remains an interested author. His banquet is artfully staged, and Socrates directs the conversation. We should therefore say that Xenophon represents Antisthenes defending a particular kind of wealth, not that a stenographer preserved Antisthenes’ exact doctrine. Even so, the representation is earlier and generically different from Diogenes Laertius’s retrospective succession. Read together, they support an Antisthenes concerned with virtue, endurance, education, friendship, and independence; they do not license transferring to him every later Cynic practice, such as systematic shamelessness, public homelessness, or the full rejection of civic convention associated with Diogenes.',
  ], ['ethics', 'cynicism'], ['socrates', 'diogenes'], ['Symposium']),
  section('ethics', 'Virtue, training, and freedom from domination', [
    'Diogenes Laertius reports a compact ethical dossier: virtue can be taught; deeds matter more than abundant words; virtue is sufficient for happiness when joined with Socratic strength; the wise person is self-sufficient; and hardship can train rather than merely injure. These propositions fit the practical emphasis of Xenophon’s scenes, but the report is late and already placed inside a Cynic-Stoic genealogy. The responsible conclusion is not that Antisthenes possessed the later Stoic system in miniature. It is that ancient evidence persistently associates him with a demanding Socratic ethics in which character and practiced capacity outweigh wealth, reputation, and theoretical display.',
    'Training, askēsis, is crucial because independence is not an attitude one acquires by repeating that externals are indifferent. Desires, fears, habits, and social comparisons exercise power through repetition; counter-training must also be repeated. Reports hostile to pleasure are best understood within this struggle against servitude, yet they should not be inflated into a demonstrated theory that every pleasure is evil. Antisthenes’ target is especially the dependence that makes judgment and action hostage to satisfaction. His ethical question is concrete: what capacities would let a person act well when money, approval, bodily ease, or political status cannot be guaranteed?',
  ], ['ethics', 'cynicism'], ['socrates', 'diogenes', 'zeno'], ['Lives of Eminent Philosophers VI']),
  section('language', 'Names, predication, and the danger of a single “logic”', [
    'Antisthenes was remembered for claims about names, definition, contradiction, and what can be said of a thing. Aristotle reports Antisthenes and his followers in debates about definition and predication, sometimes treating their position as unsophisticated. Modern reconstructions vary: some see a strict rule that a thing can be called only by its own account, some an attack on Platonic definitions, and some a dialectical challenge whose exact target is lost. The catalogue’s titles On Education or On Names, On the Use of Names, Of Questioning and Answering, and Of Opinion and Knowledge confirm sustained interest, but titles alone do not supply arguments.',
    'This material must not be blended automatically with the ethical dossier. It is tempting to claim that Antisthenes rejected theory because language cannot define essences and therefore replaced metaphysics with ethical action. That narrative may be philosophically attractive, but the connecting steps are not securely transmitted. A safer account says that he wrote extensively on discourse and was associated with provocative restrictions on contradiction or predication, while the precise semantics and motivation remain disputed. His linguistic work matters because it shows that the Socratic concern with giving an account could generate alternatives to Plato’s theory of Forms, not because one slogan yields a complete nominalist logic.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'logic', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'aristotle', 'socrates'], ['On Education or On Names', 'On the Use of Names']),
  section('literature', 'Ajax, Odysseus, and philosophical reading of poetry', [
    'Ajax and Odysseus survive as competing speeches over the arms of Achilles. Their authenticity and relationship to Antisthenes’ rhetorical education have been debated, but they are the largest continuous texts transmitted under his name. Rather than treating them as juvenilia that philosophy left behind, readers can notice how rival speakers construct merit through deeds, status, witnesses, intelligence, and service to a community. The speeches demonstrate why rhetoric and ethics cannot be separated merely by assigning them to early and late phases for which no secure chronology survives.',
    'The catalogue also attributes numerous Homeric and mythological works to Antisthenes, including writings on Heracles, Odysseus, Penelope, Circe, and the Cyclops. Later testimony associates him with distinctions between what a poet says according to opinion and according to truth, but the extent and wording of his interpretive method remain contested. Heracles could exemplify strength, labor, or education; Odysseus could exemplify versatile intelligence. These figures suggest that Antisthenes used traditional poetry as material for ethical and linguistic inquiry. They do not prove one allegorical system or permit reconstructed contents to be narrated as surviving books.',
  ], ['aesthetics', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['gorgias', 'socrates'], ['Ajax', 'Odysseus', 'Heracles or Of Wisdom or Strength']),
  section('cynicism', 'Founder, precursor, or retrospectively adopted ancestor?', [
    'Diogenes Laertius places Antisthenes at the head of a continuous succession: Socrates, Antisthenes, Diogenes, Crates, and ultimately Zeno. This genealogy gave Cynicism and Stoicism a Socratic ancestry. Yet earlier evidence does not securely call Antisthenes a Cynic or prove a teacher-pupil relationship with Diogenes of Sinope, and modern specialists dispute whether the succession is historical. The strongest wording is therefore double: Antisthenes is unquestionably a Socratic whose themes could nourish Cynic ethics, and his classification as founder or formal member of a Cynic school is a later, contested construction.',
    'Calling him a precursor does not make the connection empty. Poverty interpreted as freedom, endurance, virtue sufficient for happiness, contempt for reputation, and emphasis on deeds all overlap with later Cynic practice. But Diogenes radicalized these materials through public provocation, shamelessness, exile, and a deliberately visible life. Cynicism may be better understood as a movement that retrospectively recognized Antisthenic resources than as an institution founded on a documented day. That account preserves both continuity and difference while refusing the museum-label shortcut in which one founder single-handedly creates a school and personally transmits every doctrine to a successor.',
  ], ['cynicism', 'stoicism', 'ethics'], ['socrates', 'diogenes', 'zeno'], ['Lives of Eminent Philosophers VI']),
  section('politics', 'Convention, reputation, and civic life', [
    'Later doxography says the wise person will conduct public life according to the law of virtue rather than established laws. Combined with contempt for bad reputation and non-Athenian maternal ancestry, this can sound like a fully developed opposition between nature and convention. The evidence is too thin for that certainty. Antisthenes wrote works titled On Law or On a Commonwealth, Freedom and Slavery, and Cyrus or Of Sovereignty, but their contents are lost. The titles show political reflection; they do not establish whether he rejected civic participation, proposed an alternative constitution, or used kingship and slavery mainly as ethical metaphors.',
    'A measured interpretation centers freedom. Wealth, office, ancestry, and applause can become external masters when a person treats them as conditions of a worthwhile life. Antisthenes’ Socratic inheritance contests that authority by locating nobility in virtue and capability rather than birth. This outlook can criticize civic hierarchy without yielding a modern egalitarian program, and it can loosen allegiance to convention without proving later Cynic cosmopolitanism. The historical significance is a shift in the scale of evaluation: the city’s awards and exclusions face judgment by the trained character of the person, even though the exact political conclusions Antisthenes drew remain unrecoverable.',
  ], ['political-philosophy', 'ethics', 'cynicism'], ['socrates', 'diogenes'], ['On Law or On a Commonwealth', 'Freedom and Slavery']),
  section('rival-inheritances', 'Plato, Isocrates, and rival forms of education', [
    'Ancient reports connect Antisthenes with polemics against Plato and Isocrates, and the catalogue includes a work whose insulting title may target Plato. Such evidence indicates rivalry within fourth-century Athenian education, but it does not authorize a tidy opposition between Platonic metaphysics and Antisthenic anti-theory. Antisthenes wrote too much about names, knowledge, nature, literature, and argument to be described as uninterested in thought. His practical priority is better read as a demand that discourse answer to the formation of capable agents, not as permission to abandon reasoning.',
    'The rival Socratic authors also selected different dimensions of their shared predecessor. Plato developed dialogues that investigate definition, knowledge, soul, and political order; Xenophon emphasized useful conduct and leadership; Antisthenes appears to have joined dialogue, rhetoric, Homeric interpretation, and austere ethics. Later school histories exaggerate clean lines because genealogies make intellectual change easy to remember. A historically responsible comparison asks how distinct authors fashioned Socratic authority after Socrates’ death. It does not assign each an exclusive piece of the historical Socrates or assume that every apparent disagreement records a face-to-face quarrel.',
    'This approach also changes how influence is described. A lost Antisthenic dialogue could affect later readers without a formal school existing, and a later Cynic could resemble Antisthenes through shared admiration for Socrates rather than personal instruction. Conversely, the absence of a secure teacher chain does not prove that his writings were irrelevant to Cynic or Stoic formation. Influence here is a field of textual availability, recurring ethical exemplars, and later acts of appropriation. It is not a single line that can be inferred from similarity alone.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['plato', 'socrates'], ['Lost writings and testimonia']),
  section('reading', 'How to read an author who survives as a dossier', [
    'Begin with Xenophon’s Symposium, especially the fourth chapter, and identify what the dramatic Antisthenes values before turning those scenes into propositions. Next read the Antisthenes section of Diogenes Laertius, dividing the page into biography, sayings, doctrinal summary, catalogue, and succession narrative. These parts have different evidential force. Ajax and Odysseus then reveal a literary author whom the image of the ragged Cynic can conceal. A critical collection such as Susan Prince’s is essential for seeing where each fragment was preserved and how much surrounding context belongs to the later witness.',
    'The best final question is not whether Antisthenes “really was” a Cynic in the absence of a surviving membership register. Ask instead which Antisthenic themes are early and well attested, how later Cynics transformed them, and why ancient historians wanted a continuous Socratic succession. Keep ethics, language, literature, and logic adjacent but do not manufacture causal bridges between them. Antisthenes then emerges neither as a footnote to Socrates nor as a convenient founder label: he is a prolific, mostly lost Socratic author whose reception became one contested route from classical philosophical writing to Hellenistic philosophy as a practiced life.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'cynicism', 'ethics'], ['socrates', 'diogenes', 'plato'], ['Symposium', 'Ajax', 'Odysseus']),
];

