const carneadesSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('recovery', 'An oral philosopher behind layers of interpretation', [
    'Carneades of Cyrene became the most formidable second-century BCE leader of the skeptical Academy, but he wrote no books. His arguments were remembered by students, above all Clitomachus, whose extensive writings are themselves lost. Cicero used those Academic sources; Sextus Empiricus later organized related material from a Pyrrhonian perspective; Diogenes Laertius preserved biography and anecdotes. The result is layered transmission: we often know a Ciceronian presentation of a student’s interpretation of an oral performance. Precision about source mediation is therefore part of the philosophy, not an optional historical preface.',
    'Carneades also argued for opposed positions, making attribution unusually difficult. A theory he invented and defended may have been designed to defeat a Stoic claim by showing that another explanation fits shared assumptions; it need not express his conviction. His students already disagreed about whether he permitted the wise person to hold opinions. This profile treats his attacks and constructive alternatives as Carneadean arguments while reserving judgment about which were personal doctrines. “Probabilism” remains a useful label only if it does not turn a contested practice of persuasive appearances into a settled modern theory of probability.',
    'Transmission through Clitomachus complicates even apparent first-hand continuity. He heard Carneades and wrote hundreds of books, but later authors selected from that corpus, and Clitomachus openly interpreted his teacher’s stance toward assent. When Cicero presents two Academic readings, the disagreement is not a late modern invention imposed on clear evidence; it belongs to the ancient reception nearest the school. A responsible article therefore cites the interpretive branch rather than silently deciding that the most systematic surviving account must have been Carneades’ own final view.',
  ], ['skepticism', 'epistemology', 'platonism'], ['arcesilaus', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Academica', 'Against the Logicians']),
  section('life-academy', 'Cyrene, Athens, and leadership of the Academy', [
    'Ancient chronology places Carneades’ life around 214 to 129 or 128 BCE. Born at Cyrene in Greek-speaking North Africa, he studied in Athens, including with the Stoic Diogenes of Babylon, and became head of Plato’s Academy before 155 BCE. Reports connect his intense engagement with Chrysippus to a remark that without Chrysippus there would have been no Carneades. The saying captures intellectual dependence through opposition, but it is an anecdote, not proof that one opponent explains the whole development of his thought.',
    'The Academy he led was institutionally continuous with Plato’s school and with the skeptical turn associated with Arcesilaus. Ancient taxonomies variously call Carneades founder of a Third or New Academy, while other schemes call the entire period from Arcesilaus the New Academy. These labels mark perceived changes in method or doctrine, not a refounding of the physical institution. Carneades broadened the range and sophistication of Academic argument, but “founder” language should remain attached to the classificatory tradition rather than presented as a simple institutional fact.',
  ], ['platonism', 'skepticism', 'ancient-greek'], ['plato', 'arcesilaus', 'chrysippus'], ['Lives of Eminent Philosophers IV']),
  section('rome', 'The embassy to Rome and the unstable story of justice', [
    'In 155 BCE Athens sent Carneades, Diogenes of Babylon, and the Peripatetic Critolaus to Rome concerning a financial penalty. That embassy is the secure anchor of Carneades’ public biography. Much later sources add that he gave brilliant speeches defending justice on one day and attacking it the next, alarming Cato and hastening the philosophers’ departure. Scholars dispute the details, including how closely the fragmentary third book of Cicero’s Republic reflects those performances. The episode should illustrate his later reputation for arguing both sides, not function as a transcript.',
    'The philosophical issue remains important even when the spectacle is qualified. A defense of justice can appeal to law, mutual advantage, and civic order; an attack can ask whether states and individuals actually sacrifice advantage when justice demands it. Presenting both cases tests whether customary confidence survives counterargument. It does not prove that Carneades was personally unjust, nihilistic, or secretly committed to whichever speech came second. Academic opposition aimed to expose dependence on unexamined premises and to train judgment by making an attractive case confront an attractive rival.',
  ], ['political-philosophy', 'ethics', 'skepticism'], ['chrysippus'], ['On the Republic III']),
  section('cognition', 'Continuing the attack on cognitive impressions', [
    'Carneades inherited the Academic challenge to the Stoic cognitive impression, the supposedly truth-guaranteeing presentation recognizable as something false could not produce. Later reports distinguish attacks on its special clarity from attacks based on indiscernible objects. Twins, similar eggs, seals, dreams, and abnormal states make the same pressure vivid: the mind may confront a false appearance no less convincing than a true one. If internal character cannot certify the right causal history, assent cannot become infallible simply because an impression feels exceptionally clear.',
    'These arguments do not entail that every impression is equally useful or every claim false. Their target is a criterion promising secure discrimination and the Stoic ideal of wisdom built upon it. Carneades could grant that many impressions arise accurately and still deny that accuracy carries an unmistakable guarantee. This gap between truth and warranted recognition motivates his account of persuasive guidance. It also preserves the dialectical question: he may be offering an alternative psychology because the Stoics claimed no action was possible without cognition, rather than announcing his own final epistemology.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'stoicism', 'skepticism'], ['arcesilaus', 'zeno', 'chrysippus'], ['Academica II', 'Against the Logicians VII']),
  section('persuasive', 'Pithanon: persuasive, plausible, or “probable”?', [
    'Cicero renders the Greek pithanon with Latin probabilis, which explains the traditional label “probable impression.” Modern English “probability” can wrongly suggest numerical likelihood or a formal calculus. The basic idea is an impression that appears persuasive or approval-worthy. An initial appearance can guide a low-stakes action without claiming to reveal its own truth. For greater importance, a person can check whether it coheres with accompanying impressions and whether the conditions of observation are undisturbed. The surviving account thus describes graded scrutiny rather than certainty.',
    'Sextus reports a three-level procedure: an impression may be persuasive; persuasive and not contradicted by associated appearances; or additionally examined in its relevant parts and circumstances. The terminology and systematization may owe something to Carneades’ students and later presentation. It should not be narrated as a handbook written by Carneades. Still, the central insight is well supported: fallible inquiry can scale effort to stakes. A quick everyday choice and a grave decision need not demand identical checking, and neither requires pretending that the final appearance is incapable of being false.',
    'Checking is relational rather than magical. A visible object is considered alongside distance, lighting, the observer’s condition, memories, and other concurrent appearances; contradiction can reduce confidence even when no single appearance provides certainty. Examination does not convert persuasion into Stoic cognition, because a thoroughly checked impression may still be false. Its achievement is practical discrimination under acknowledged risk. That distinction makes the proposal more demanding than “follow whatever seems right” and more modest than “evidence eventually guarantees truth.”',
  ], ['epistemology', 'skepticism', 'ethics'], ['arcesilaus', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Against the Logicians VII.159–189', 'Academica II']),
  section('assent', 'Approval, assent, and disagreement among his students', [
    'The practical proposal created a problem. If the skeptic follows a persuasive impression, has that person assented and formed an opinion? Cicero reports two interpretations. Clitomachus understood Carneades to preserve suspension: the skeptic can follow or approve an appearance without taking it to be true. Metrodorus of Stratonicea and later Philo were associated with a view on which the wise person may hold qualified opinions while recognizing the absence of cognition. Because both claimed Carneadean authority, the dispute reaches back close to his oral teaching.',
    'This is why it is unsafe to say simply that Carneades replaced strict skepticism with probabilistic belief. He certainly furnished tools later Academics could use that way. He may also have defended permission to opine in some contexts. But he may have offered both alternatives dialectically or maintained the Clitomachean distinction between practical approval and truth-committing assent. The historical uncertainty is philosophically revealing: acting on an appearance, treating it as a working basis, and believing it true can come apart conceptually even if critics doubt that ordinary psychology can keep them separate.',
    'Cicero’s Latin terms add another layer. Academic approval, following, assent, opinion, and cognition occupy a technical debate shaped first in Greek and then reconstructed for Roman readers. English summaries can collapse them all into “belief,” making the dispute disappear. The important contrast is functional: one mental response authorizes action while remaining revisable; another claims the appearance as true; cognition adds the stronger security prized by the Stoics. Exactly where Carneades drew those boundaries remains contested, but the boundaries themselves organize the surviving controversy.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'skepticism'], ['arcesilaus'], ['Academica II.59, 67, 78, 98–104']),
  section('ethics', 'Arguments over the human end', [
    'Carneades surveyed competing theories of the human good and organized them by what they treated as the primary object of natural pursuit: pleasure, absence of pain, natural advantages, virtue, or combinations of these. Cicero’s ethical works preserve a taxonomy that became influential, but scholars dispute which parts are Carneades’ own classification and which reflect later Academic systematization. Its skeptical function is clear. By displaying nearby alternatives, the taxonomy asks each school to justify why its favored end uniquely captures what humans naturally seek and ought rationally to choose.',
    'Carneades sometimes defended an end described as enjoying the primary things according to nature. That proposal may have been constructed to oppose the Stoics by retaining natural advantages without making virtue the only good; it need not be his personal ethics. The pattern matches his broader method: construct a credible alternative that meets shared considerations and see whether an opponent can still claim uniqueness. Consequently, profiles should not list “Carneadean ethics” as if a surviving treatise fixed its doctrine. What is secure is a powerful comparative criticism of dogmatic ethical theories and their accounts of natural motivation.',
  ], ['ethics', 'skepticism', 'stoicism'], ['chrysippus', 'epicurus', 'aristotle'], ['On Ends V']),
  section('fate-theology', 'Fate, divination, and the gods as dialectical targets', [
    'Cicero’s incomplete On Fate makes Carneades an important participant in debates over determinism, voluntary action, bivalence, and causal necessity. He attacked both Stoic fate and Epicurean solutions, but extracting a positive theory is difficult. An argument that a free action needs no antecedent external cause may defend agency against Chrysippus without committing Carneades to a complete metaphysics of causation. Later summaries compress a long debate, and the missing parts of Cicero’s dialogue limit confidence about sequence and ownership.',
    'Arguments concerning gods and divination require the same caution. Carneades used sorites-style progressions to pressure Stoic definitions of divinity: if acknowledged divine cases justify increasingly similar cases, the category expands toward absurdity. Cicero says the aim was to expose inadequate Stoic explanation rather than abolish the gods, but that statement belongs to Cicero’s Academic priest Cotta and may not reveal Carneades’ private religious attitude. Calling Carneades an atheist, a fideist, or an agnostic exceeds the evidence. The secure claim is that he made theological classification answer to consistent criteria.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-religion', 'logic'], ['chrysippus', 'epicurus'], ['On Fate', 'On the Nature of the Gods III']),
  section('method-legacy', 'Argument on both sides without indifference to truth', [
    'Carneadean practice is sometimes mistaken for the trick of making any claim sound equally good. Ancient evidence also describes argument on both sides as a way to discover truth or reveal what remains persuasive after criticism. Dialectic can be intellectually serious without promising final possession: it identifies hidden costs, tests criteria, and disciplines confidence. Carneades’ constructive alternatives mattered because they showed that a dominant system was not the only account capable of explaining shared experience. Their value need not depend on his secretly believing each one.',
    'His successors turned this practice into divergent Academic positions. Clitomachus emphasized suspension; Metrodorus and Philo allowed more room for qualified belief; Antiochus eventually rejected the skeptical Academy. Cicero transmitted these disputes to Latin readers, where probabilis acquired a long afterlife in accounts of reasonable judgment. Later reception often systematized “Carneadean probabilism” more cleanly than the oral evidence warrants. His most defensible legacy is not one probability doctrine but a model of inquiry that combines relentless counterargument with graded, revisable practical confidence.',
  ], ['skepticism', 'epistemology', 'platonism'], ['arcesilaus'], ['Academica']),
  section('reading', 'How to read Carneades without assigning every argument to him', [
    'Start with Cicero’s Academica II, especially its discussions of cognitive impressions, persuasive guidance, and the disagreement about opinion. Use a specialist guide alongside it because the dialogue’s speakers represent competing Academic histories. Then compare Sextus, Against the Logicians 7.159–189, whose orderly presentation of persuasive impressions is invaluable but later and Pyrrhonian. Diogenes Laertius 4.62–66 supplies a compact life and the claim that Carneades wrote nothing. Cicero’s On Fate and On the Nature of the Gods broaden the dossier while introducing further dramatic speakers and textual loss.',
    'At each stage label the source, speaker, target, and level of commitment. Translate pithanon as “persuasive” before importing modern probabilistic associations. Treat the Roman justice speeches as a disputed reception episode, not recorded orations. Distinguish a theory Carneades devised from a doctrine he endorsed, and distinguish his students’ systematic interpretations from secure personal teaching. Read this way, Carneades offers a demanding lesson for ordinary reasoning: lack of certainty need not collapse inquiry, because appearances can be compared, checked, ranked, and followed provisionally while the possibility of error remains visible.',
    'Finally, compare the sources rather than harmonizing them. Cicero preserves Academic self-interpretation through dramatic dialogue; Sextus compares the Academy with his own Pyrrhonism; Diogenes collects memorable biography. When they disagree, the disagreement is evidence about reception and school identity. Carneades becomes historically clearer when the temptation to give him the single polished system that none of these witnesses can securely deliver is resisted.',
  ], ['skepticism', 'epistemology', 'ethics'], ['arcesilaus', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Academica II', 'Against the Logicians VII', 'On Fate']),
];

